  "title": "What plane glue does",
  "status": "Proved",
  "claim": "What plane glue does (computed). On BRACE-TREE C's 111 fresh spaces and 80 random spaces with plane glue (Fano atoms hung on random plane spaces or on census spaces), with exact creation maps: wherever only Fano atoms lie between a removal and an atom, the removal's side leaves the facing cut's ports free and the touching rule holds; through glue the side is free, excludes one pattern pair, or ties two ports (a doubled square can make two ports one point), and the touching rule holds at every pair whose side is free, failing only where glue constrains the ports, as ticket A's law explains. The twin rule's two disturbances are local: every glue line that joins an atom's twin class is a doubled-square line meeting the atom's cuts at two or more ports (a point that is a port of two cuts counting for each), and every atom that splits emits a line into a planar brace and splits into its lines through each point of that line, the classes' out-sets differing only on glue lines.",
